\title{Arithmetic on the M\"obius Band:\\Quotient Obstructions, Collapsed Coordinates, and a Graded Repair}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze a proposed arithmetic on the M\"obius band obtained from $[0,1]\times\mathbb R$ by the endpoint identification $(0,y)\sim(1,-y)$. The proposal combines coordinatewise addition and multiplication with the integer placement
\[
n\longmapsto\left(\frac12+\frac{1}{2n},|n|\right)
\]
and scalar evaluation $V(x,y)=y(2x-1)$. Although the endpoint rule is an equivalence relation, neither coordinatewise operation respects it, so neither descends to the quotient. Moreover, the proposed evaluation maps every positive integer to $1$ and every negative integer to $-1$. Finally, the proposed orientation factor $-1$ is a unit rather than a prime; $-6=(-2)(-3)$ is false; and zero has no finite factorization into nonzero integer factors. We formulate a corrected program based on a ring bundle or a $\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z$-graded algebra in which twisted times twisted is untwisted. The analysis illustrates the general requirement that operations on quotient spaces be independent of representatives.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The M\"obius band is the elementary non-orientable surface obtained by gluing the ends of a strip after a half-turn. A path around its central circle reverses a transverse orientation. This makes the band an attractive setting for speculative arithmetic: one might hope to represent sign geometrically, arrange for a circuit to change a positive quantity into a negative one, and treat orientation as a distinguished factor.

A proposed construction must answer three logically separate questions. First, what equivalence relation defines the quotient? Second, do the proposed operations depend only on quotient points rather than on chosen representatives? Third, does the proposed placement of integers preserve the values and factorizations it is intended to represent? The order is essential. Units, primes, zero divisors, and integral domains presuppose well-defined addition and multiplication.

Let $S=[0,1]\times\mathbb R$. The endpoint gluing is
\[
(0,y)\sim(1,-y).
\]
We first make this relation explicit, then test coordinatewise arithmetic. The counterexamples reveal a structural mismatch: two sign reversals in two inputs cancel in their product, whereas the quotient seam contributes a single reversal. This failure points toward the correct replacement. Twisted quantities should form the odd component of a graded algebra, and the product of two odd elements should lie in an untwisted even component.

\section{The endpoint quotient}

\begin{definition}[Raw points and endpoint relation]
A raw point is a pair $(x,y)\in\mathbb R^2$. Define $(x,y)\sim(u,v)$ when at least one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $(x,y)=(u,v)$;
\item $x=0$, $u=1$, and $y=-v$;
\item $x=1$, $u=0$, and $y=-v$.
\end{enumerate}
For the geometric strip the first coordinate is restricted to $[0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Endpoint equivalence]
The relation $\sim$ is an equivalence relation. In particular, for every $y\in\mathbb R$,
\[
(0,y)\sim(1,-y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Reflexivity follows from clause (i). Symmetry exchanges clauses (ii) and (iii). For transitivity, any equality case is immediate. A nontrivial chain that crosses from one edge to the other and back changes the fiber sign twice, so the first and third raw points are equal. These cases exhaust the defining clauses.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Same-edge opposites remain distinct]
The points $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are not equivalent.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
They are unequal, and neither cross-edge clause applies because both first coordinates are $0$.
\end{proof}

Thus the quotient $S/{\sim}$ is legitimate, but the relation is specific: it does not identify every pair obtained by reversing a sign.

\section{Descent of operations}

\begin{definition}[Representative independence]
Let $F:S\times S\to S$ be a binary function. It is representative-independent if
\[
a\sim a'\text{ and }b\sim b'\quad\Longrightarrow\quad F(a,b)\sim F(a',b')
\]
for all raw points $a,a',b,b'$.
\end{definition}

This condition is necessary and sufficient for the formula
\[
\overline F([a],[b])=[F(a,b)]
\]
to define a binary operation on equivalence classes. Necessity follows because equal quotient inputs must have equal quotient outputs. Sufficiency follows because the condition guarantees that changing either representative preserves the output class.

\subsection{Multiplication}

Consider the coordinatewise product
\[
(x,y)\odot(u,v)=(xu,yv).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Multiplication obstruction]
Coordinatewise multiplication is not representative-independent and therefore does not induce multiplication on the M\"obius endpoint quotient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set
\[
a=b=(0,1),\qquad a'=b'=(1,-1).
\]
Then $a\sim a'$ and $b\sim b'$. However,
\[
a\odot b=(0,1),\qquad a'\odot b'=(1,1).
\]
For these outputs to be equivalent, the endpoint rule would require the second fiber coordinate to be the negative of the first; this would require $1=-1$. Hence the outputs are inequivalent.
\end{proof}

The geometric content is that moving each input across the seam introduces two sign reversals, which cancel:
\[
(-y)(-v)=yv.
\]
The output is therefore untwisted. Attempting to place it back in a single twisted sector is incompatible with the seam.

\begin{corollary}[No coordinatewise quotient multiplication]
The endpoint quotient cannot be a ring with multiplication induced by the displayed coordinatewise product. In particular, a product calculated from representatives cannot establish the existence of zero divisors on this quotient.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A ring multiplication must first be a well-defined binary operation on the carrier. The preceding theorem rules this out for the proposed product.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Addition}

Consider coordinatewise addition,
\[
(x,y)\oplus(u,v)=(x+u,y+v).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Addition obstruction]
Coordinatewise addition is not representative-independent and therefore does not induce addition on the M\"obius endpoint quotient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Using the same equivalent representatives gives
\[
a\oplus b=(0,2),\qquad a'\oplus b'=(2,-2).
\]
The outputs are unequal and satisfy neither endpoint clause, since their first coordinates are $0$ and $2$ rather than opposite endpoints $0$ and $1$.
\end{proof}

There is also a closure problem: adding base coordinates can leave $[0,1]$. A periodic reduction or another correction would constitute a new operation and would require its own proof of representative independence.

\begin{corollary}[Failure of the proposed ring construction]
Coordinatewise formulas do not make the M\"obius endpoint quotient a ring. Consequently they do not support conclusions about integral-domain structure or zero divisors.
\end{corollary}

\section{The proposed integer placement}

For a nonzero integer $n$, define
\[
c(n)=\frac12+\frac{1}{2n},\qquad s(n)=|n|,
\]
and assign the raw point $(c(n),s(n))$. The proposed scalar evaluation is
\[
V(x,y)=y(2x-1).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Magnitude collapse]
For every nonzero integer $n$,
\[
V(c(n),s(n))=\frac{|n|}{n}=
\begin{cases}
1,&n>0,\\
-1,&n<0.
\end{cases}
\]
Thus the proposed evaluation retains sign but not magnitude.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A direct calculation gives
\[
2c(n)-1=2\left(\frac12+\frac{1}{2n}\right)-1=\frac1n.
\]
Multiplication by $s(n)=|n|$ yields $|n|/n$. The positive and negative cases follow from the definition of absolute value.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Distinct coordinates with equal values]
The proposed points for $2$ and $3$ have distinct first coordinates, but both have evaluated value $1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
One has
\[
c(2)=\frac34\ne\frac23=c(3),
\]
while the magnitude-collapse theorem gives $V(c(2),s(2))=V(c(3),s(3))=1$.
\end{proof}

The point-valued placement and the scalar interpretation must therefore be distinguished. The pairs can separate $2$ and $3$, but the claimed real-number value cannot recover them. Moreover, $c(0)$ is undefined. The formula is not a placement of all integers unless zero is handled separately.

A claim of one-point compactification requires additional data: a topology, an injective copy of the original discrete space, a distinguished added point, compactness, and the appropriate induced topology. The displayed coordinate formula alone does not establish these properties.

\section{Factorization, units, and zero}

Once the invalid quotient operations are set aside, the numerical claims reduce to ordinary integer arithmetic.

\begin{proposition}[Factorization of six]
The valid identities are
\[
6=2\cdot3,\qquad -6=2\cdot3\cdot(-1).
\]
The identity $-6=(-2)(-3)$ is false.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Two negative signs cancel, so $(-2)(-3)=6$. An additional factor of $-1$ produces $-6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Integer unit]
An integer $u$ is a unit if there is an integer $v$ such that $uv=1$. A prime integer is required to be a nonunit in addition to satisfying its divisibility condition.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Orientation-unit theorem]
The only integer units are $1$ and $-1$. Consequently $-1$ is a unit and not a prime.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $uv=1$, then taking absolute values gives $|u||v|=1$. Both factors are nonnegative integers, so $|u|=1$, and hence $u=1$ or $u=-1$. Conversely, each of $1$ and $-1$ is its own inverse. Since primes are nonunits, $-1$ is not prime.
\end{proof}

The algebraic role of orientation is thus reversible: multiplication by $-1$ reverses sign and $(-1)^2=1$. This is the behavior of an order-two unit, not of irreducible magnitude.

\begin{theorem}[Zero has no nonzero finite factorization]
If $a_1,\ldots,a_k$ are nonzero integers, then
\[
a_1a_2\cdots a_k\ne0.
\]
Therefore zero cannot be a finite product of nonzero prime factors.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The integers have no zero divisors. Induction on the number of factors shows that a finite product of nonzero integers is nonzero. The empty product is $1$, so it does not provide an exception.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact computational diagnostics}

The mathematical tests above lead to several useful algorithms.

\subsection{Endpoint test}

Given two rational raw points, first test equality. If they differ, test whether their base coordinates are opposite endpoints and whether their fiber coordinates sum to zero. This uses a constant number of exact comparisons and arithmetic operations, so its arithmetic-operation complexity is $O(1)$ and its auxiliary storage is $O(1)$. Bit complexity depends on the sizes of the rational numerators and denominators.

\subsection{Counterexample test for descent}

Given a candidate operation $F$, choose related inputs $a\sim a'$ and $b\sim b'$, calculate both outputs, and run the endpoint test. An inequivalent output pair conclusively refutes descent. The fixed witness $(0,1)\sim(1,-1)$ refutes both coordinatewise operations. Passing finitely many examples does not prove universal compatibility, but one failed example is decisive.

\subsection{Evaluation audit}

For each selected nonzero integer $n$, exact rational arithmetic computes $c(n)$ and $V(c(n),|n|)$. Symbolic simplification proves the general formula $|n|/n$; a numerical table illustrates the collapse without floating-point error.

\subsection{Signed factorization}

For $n\ne0$, separate the unit sign from the magnitude:
\[
n=\operatorname{sgn}(n)|n|,
\qquad \operatorname{sgn}(n)\in\{1,-1\}.
\]
Then factor $|n|$. Trial division checks candidates up to $\sqrt{|n|}$ and takes $O(\sqrt{|n|})$ divisions in the worst case. The input $0$ must be reported separately because it has no finite nonzero prime factorization.

\section{Corrected geometric algebra}

\subsection{Fiberwise addition}

The M\"obius band is the total space of a real line bundle over the circle. Each fiber is a vector space, so addition is natural for two vectors over the same base point. There is no canonical addition of vectors over different base points without extra data, such as a connection or trivialization. This explains why globally adding total-space coordinates is geometrically unnatural.

\subsection{The tensor-square repair}

Let $L$ be the M\"obius line bundle. A product of two quantities transforming like sections of $L$ transforms like a section of $L\otimes L$. Each factor changes sign across the seam, so the product does not:
\[
(-y)(-v)=yv.
\]
Thus $L\otimes L$ is untwisted. The original operation failed because it forced this untwisted product back into the twisted total space.

\subsection{A graded algebra}

A natural replacement is
\[
A=A_0\oplus A_1,
\]
where $A_0$ is untwisted and $A_1$ is twisted, with multiplication satisfying
\[
A_iA_j\subseteq A_{i+j\bmod 2}.
\]
Explicitly,
\[
A_0A_0\subseteq A_0,\qquad
A_0A_1\subseteq A_1,\qquad
A_1A_1\subseteq A_0.
\]
The last inclusion is precisely the two-twists-cancel law. Orientation may be recorded as degree $1$ in $\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z$, or by a unit $\tau$ with $\tau^2=1$. Both models capture reversibility and avoid misclassifying orientation as prime.

\subsection{Transported ring structures}

A ring structure can be transported to a bare set $X$ through a bijection $f:X\to R$ with a known ring:
\[
x\boxplus y=f^{-1}(f(x)+f(y)),\qquad
x\boxtimes y=f^{-1}(f(x)f(y)).
\]
This guarantees the ring axioms set-theoretically. It does not guarantee geometric naturality, continuity, compatibility with fibers, or canonicity. Any arithmetic intended to express M\"obius geometry must impose and prove such extra properties.

\section{Applications and broader context}

Representative independence is a universal quotient principle. Arithmetic on residue classes must respect congruence; functions on projective space must ignore rescaling; and physical observables in a gauge theory must not depend on gauge representatives. In every case, a quotient removes redundant descriptions, and a legitimate operation must ignore the redundancy.

The graded correction also appears broadly. Fermionic parity separates even and odd sectors, with odd times odd becoming even. Orientation lines track signs under coordinate changes. Group rings and crossed products encode symmetries as actions and invertible elements. These analogies support the conclusion that orientation is naturally a grading or unit.

The distinction between units and primes is central to factorization. Prime factors record noninvertible multiplicative content, while units record reversible ambiguity. Unique factorization is unique only up to reordering and multiplication by units. Sign is therefore already part of ordinary integer factorization, but it appears in exactly the role established here.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The proposed construction combines topology, algebra, and arithmetic, but its requirements should be separated. A corrected program should:
\begin{enumerate}
\item define the topological space and prove any compactification claim;
\item define candidate operations and prove closure and representative independence;
\item establish the ring axioms;
\item define the integer map and prove injectivity or scalar value recovery;
\item only then classify units, zero divisors, irreducibles, and primes.
\end{enumerate}

The most promising direction is a graded algebra attached to the M\"obius line bundle. One should construct the twisted and untwisted components, define all mixed products, and prove associativity. A second direction begins with the standard one-point compactification of the discrete integers and asks whether it admits a geometrically meaningful embedding into a M\"obius-related space. A third studies orientation through representations, tensor products, and parity rather than primality. Finally, one may classify affine or polynomial operations on representatives that satisfy the endpoint compatibility law.

\section{Conclusion}

The endpoint identification $(0,y)\sim(1,-y)$ defines a valid quotient, but coordinatewise addition and multiplication do not descend to it. Equivalent inputs yield inequivalent outputs. The proposed scalar interpretation of integer points sends every positive integer to $1$ and every negative integer to $-1$. The correct ordinary identities are $6=2\cdot3$ and $-6=2\cdot3\cdot(-1)$; the factor $-1$ is an order-two unit rather than a prime, and zero has no finite product decomposition into nonzero primes.

The obstruction supplies the constructive lesson. A twist is not magnitude but parity. Two twisted factors produce an untwisted product, so a bundle-aware or $\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z$-graded algebra is the natural setting. The M\"obius band remains a productive guide to arithmetic precisely because its geometry reveals which algebraic structures are compatible with non-orientability.

